WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.376 --> 00:00:03.586
This is an F-16 fighter jet.

00:00:03.586 --> 00:00:05.714
Since the 1970s, it's been 
a cornerstone

00:00:05.714 --> 00:00:07.090
of the US Air Force.

00:00:07.132 --> 00:00:10.468
Now the US allows all these 
militaries to also have F-16s.

00:00:10.927 --> 00:00:13.388
And since Russia invaded 
Ukraine in 2022...

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:17.058
Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy
has been begging for them.

00:00:17.058 --> 00:00:19.811
“Zelenskyy has been doubling down 
on his request for...”

00:00:19.811 --> 00:00:20.854
“...NATO jets...”

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:23.106
“...and they really are pleas 
for fighter jets.”

00:00:23.106 --> 00:00:26.067
But for Ukraine, the US 
hasn't been as generous.

00:00:26.067 --> 00:00:27.444
“You don't think he needs 
F-16s now?”

00:00:27.444 --> 00:00:29.237
“No, He doesn't need
F-16s now.”

00:00:29.237 --> 00:00:30.488
And that might be surprising...

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:34.117
because the US has already given 
nearly $47 billion

00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:36.411
in military aid to Ukraine 
over the past year.

00:00:36.828 --> 00:00:38.413
That's a lot of weapons.

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:42.000
Far more than it gave even some 
of its closest allies in 2020...

00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:43.918
and more than four times

00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:47.213
what it gave Afghanistan's army at the
height of the US-led war there.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.549
This is a historic number that 
you would see 

00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:53.470
typically the US military give to 
another country over decades.

00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:56.264
The U.S. refusing 
to send F-16s 

00:00:56.264 --> 00:00:57.766
is a choice worth understanding...

00:00:58.308 --> 00:01:01.853
because the weapons that the US has 
and hasn't chosen to send Ukraine 

00:01:01.853 --> 00:01:03.229
and when they've sent them

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.273
have helped shape each phase 
of this war.

00:01:07.025 --> 00:01:10.111
So what has the US 
been giving to Ukraine

00:01:10.111 --> 00:01:10.779
and why?

00:01:23.041 --> 00:01:24.209
Back in 2014

00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:26.961
Russia took over Crimea and 
parts of eastern Ukraine

00:01:26.961 --> 00:01:28.671
with the help of Ukrainian 
separatist fighters.

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:31.966
In 2014, ragtag wouldn't 
even describe

00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:34.135
the state of the Ukrainian 
armed forces.

00:01:34.385 --> 00:01:38.306
This is Jack Detsch — Foreign Policy’s
US  national security reporter.

00:01:38.306 --> 00:01:40.934
They'd been gutted 
by years of neglect.

00:01:41.017 --> 00:01:43.103
A lot of the troops 
didn't have uniforms.

00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:45.313
Some of them didn't 
have hot food.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.398
Ukraine's leaders asked 
the US for help

00:01:47.398 --> 00:01:48.817
but President Barack Obama

00:01:48.817 --> 00:01:51.611
only sent protective gear and supplies, 
not weapons.

00:01:52.028 --> 00:01:54.489
In 2018, President Donald trump agreed

00:01:54.489 --> 00:01:57.867
to send a limited number of anti-tank
missiles called Javelins.

00:01:57.867 --> 00:02:00.286
But the Ukrainian army was still 
woefully unprepared

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:02.539
if Russia decided to escalate the conflict...

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:03.790
which it did.

00:02:06.543 --> 00:02:10.338
In 2022, Russia launched a 
full-scale invasion of Ukraine.

00:02:12.757 --> 00:02:13.800
Russian jets and missiles

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.468
were doing immense damage 
from the air...

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:18.096
and Russian tanks were pushing 
Ukraine's troops backwards.

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:21.099
Ukraine again asked 
the US for help.

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:22.475
From the beginning of the war

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:24.310
the Ukrainians wanted 
a lot of the things 

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.146
that you see on 
the menu right now.

00:02:26.437 --> 00:02:27.897
They wanted F-16s.

00:02:27.897 --> 00:02:29.524
They wanted main 
battle tanks.

00:02:29.524 --> 00:02:31.025
They wanted this stuff 
from the get-go.

00:02:31.025 --> 00:02:33.945
President Joe Biden agreed to send 
a large package of weapons

00:02:33.945 --> 00:02:35.822
but it mainly included 
the smaller

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:37.991
and relatively unsophisticated kinds.

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:40.869
The US sent more javelins, 
plus ammunition, guns

00:02:40.869 --> 00:02:43.204
and even some anti-aircraft missiles
called Stingers.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:46.814
And many US allies sent similar 
types of weapons.

00:02:47.698 --> 00:02:50.879
Germany sent 1000 anti-tank missiles
and 500 stingers.

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:53.256
Belgium sent 
2000 machine guns.

00:02:53.256 --> 00:02:55.967
But no one sent their advanced 
battle tanks or fighter jets.

00:02:56.384 --> 00:02:58.303
The main question in the 
beginning of the war

00:02:58.303 --> 00:03:00.763
was not just what the US 
was going to send.

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:03.349
I mean, the question was like, 
are these guys going to fight?

00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:05.518
Certainly that's been 
a resounding yes.

00:03:06.102 --> 00:03:08.313
Ukraine's soldiers used 
anti-aircraft missiles

00:03:08.313 --> 00:03:10.732
to prevent Russian aircraft
from controlling the skies.

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:13.318
And they used the guns, ammo, 
and anti-tank weapons

00:03:13.318 --> 00:03:14.736
to halt Russia's progress.

00:03:16.487 --> 00:03:18.656
Like in this video, which shows 
Ukrainian soldiers

00:03:18.656 --> 00:03:21.117
destroying dozens of Russian tanks 
approaching Kiev.

00:03:22.327 --> 00:03:24.871
But even though the Ukrainians 
were using these weapons well

00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:27.332
the US hesitated to send it 
more advanced equipment.

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:28.416
So the US

00:03:28.416 --> 00:03:32.086
has been concerned at almost 
every step about the potential

00:03:32.086 --> 00:03:34.088
for Russia escalating 
the conflict.

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:36.257
The US worried that Ukraine 
might use

00:03:36.257 --> 00:03:37.508
some of these advanced weapons

00:03:37.508 --> 00:03:40.803
like fighter jets or long-range missiles 
to strike inside Russia

00:03:40.803 --> 00:03:42.472
sparking an escalation.

00:03:42.764 --> 00:03:46.017
This concern would come to define 
how the US supplied Ukraine.

00:03:50.146 --> 00:03:53.233
In the spring of 2022, the war 
entered its second phase.

00:03:53.983 --> 00:03:56.444
Russia and Ukraine's armies weren't
moving as much anymore

00:03:56.444 --> 00:03:58.821
and instead began pounding each other
with artillery.

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:02.158
Russia was firing a staggering 
60,000 rounds a day...

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:04.911
while Ukraine could only manage 
to fire around 6,000.

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:07.747
And it was quickly 
running low.

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:09.290
But the US was still hesitating

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:10.917
to expand the scope of its support.

00:04:12.126 --> 00:04:13.378
They're looking at what's

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:15.129
kind of on the Christmas 
wish list

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:17.382
and deciding what's actually 
prudent to give.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:19.425
Finally, in April 2022

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:21.803
the US agreed to send Ukraine 
its own artillery.

00:04:22.567 --> 00:04:24.264
And like in the first phase of the war

00:04:24.264 --> 00:04:26.341
a number of allies then sent 
their own artillery...

00:04:26.574 --> 00:04:28.743
even though some had 
wanted to do it earlier.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.897
You sort of saw folks in Europe 
wanting the US to lead

00:04:32.897 --> 00:04:36.484
but sometimes the US still 
very much deep in deliberation mode.

00:04:36.901 --> 00:04:39.529
The most important weapon the US 
sent were called HIMARS...

00:04:39.862 --> 00:04:41.948
powerful rocket launchers 
that could hit targets

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:43.449
as far as 80 kilometers.

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:45.785
They weren't powerful enough 
to strike inside Russia

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:48.329
but they did enable Ukraine to 
hit Russian supply depots

00:04:48.329 --> 00:04:50.623
and command posts far behind 
its front lines...

00:04:51.124 --> 00:04:53.001
which forced the Russians 
to move them back

00:04:53.001 --> 00:04:54.669
weakening its troops 
at the front.

00:04:56.379 --> 00:04:58.172
That's how Ukraine captured 
the Kharkiv region

00:04:58.172 --> 00:05:00.967
in September and the major city of Kherson 
in November.

00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:04.470
And by then, a new phase 
was beginning.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:10.393
In winter 2023, both sides were 
planning new attacks.

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:12.020
Russia was gearing up

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:14.480
to push further into the Donetsk 
and Luhansk regions.

00:05:15.106 --> 00:05:17.233
But Ukraine also wanted 
to take back territory...

00:05:17.442 --> 00:05:19.193
particularly in the south.

00:05:19.193 --> 00:05:20.153
In order to do this

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:21.779
Ukraine would need to 
move troops quickly

00:05:21.779 --> 00:05:23.948
which requires armored vehicles 
like tanks...

00:05:24.198 --> 00:05:26.242
something it had been asking for 
for months.

00:05:26.242 --> 00:05:28.453
Some countries had sent 
older models of tanks...

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:31.122
but just like with artillery, 
the US waited.

00:05:32.165 --> 00:05:33.833
They wanted the Germans 
to act first

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:35.501
by sending Leopard battle tanks.

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:36.044
The Germans...

00:05:36.085 --> 00:05:37.420
were waiting for 
the United States

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.630
to give them some political 
top cover here.

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:41.132
A little bit of 
a game of chicken.

00:05:41.132 --> 00:05:43.051
Finally, in January 2023

00:05:43.051 --> 00:05:45.762
the UK and then the US agreed 
to send their advanced tanks...

00:05:46.815 --> 00:05:48.901
prompting Germany and 
several allies to follow

00:05:48.901 --> 00:05:50.110
with their best battle tanks.

00:05:50.391 --> 00:05:53.186
Just like with anti-tank missiles
and HIMARS before 

00:05:53.186 --> 00:05:54.687
the US and its allies 
were hoping that

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:56.356
these tanks can push Ukraine 
to win

00:05:56.356 --> 00:05:58.483
some decisive victories 
in this phase...

00:05:58.733 --> 00:06:00.651
which is where we are now.

00:06:00.651 --> 00:06:03.237
If the Ukrainians kind of 
can't turn the tide

00:06:03.237 --> 00:06:05.615
turn the balance right now

00:06:05.615 --> 00:06:06.908
it's going to be tricky

00:06:06.908 --> 00:06:11.412
to see how this doesn't go into 
an extended stalemate.

00:06:11.412 --> 00:06:13.873
That's why Ukraine is still 
asking for fighter jets.

00:06:14.499 --> 00:06:17.210
F-16s could protect Ukraine's tanks 
from Russian aircraft...

00:06:17.418 --> 00:06:19.462
making their attack 
much more powerful.

00:06:20.171 --> 00:06:21.714
Ukraine has also 
been asking the US

00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:23.841
for a long range missiles 
called ATACMS

00:06:23.841 --> 00:06:29.138
which it could use to strike Russian positions as far as 
300 kilometers behind their lines.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:32.183
Already, a few allies are in favor of 
sending these weapons.

00:06:32.183 --> 00:06:33.601
But as of this video

00:06:33.601 --> 00:06:36.396
the US is hesitating over 
familiar concerns.

00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:39.816
Questions about their readiness 
for training on those.

00:06:39.857 --> 00:06:42.402
Also, of course, you know, 
just the question again...

00:06:42.652 --> 00:06:45.196
how far are the Ukrainians
going to fire these things?

00:06:45.196 --> 00:06:47.782
So far, none of the decisions 
to send any of these weapons

00:06:47.782 --> 00:06:49.242
has inspired Russia to escalate.

00:06:49.367 --> 00:06:51.369
And in March, Poland
and Slovakia

00:06:51.369 --> 00:06:53.413
agreed to send some of their 
older fighter jets

00:06:53.413 --> 00:06:55.540
making the eventual 
approval of F-16s

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:57.708
or long range missiles 
more of a possibility.

00:06:58.334 --> 00:07:01.963
But after giving Ukraine over 
$46 billion in military aid

00:07:01.963 --> 00:07:04.465
public opinion in the US 
is changing.

00:07:04.632 --> 00:07:06.259
A recent Pew Research poll showed

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:10.012
the proportion of people who believe the US
has sent too much aid to Ukraine has grown

00:07:10.012 --> 00:07:11.806
particularly on the right.

00:07:11.806 --> 00:07:14.016
Biden, facing a lot of pressure

00:07:14.016 --> 00:07:15.935
not only within his party, 
but, of course

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:19.439
from Republican rivals getting ready 
for the 2024 debate stage.

00:07:19.522 --> 00:07:22.275
That's going to make it much more difficult
for President Biden

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:24.152
to sustain this clip.

00:07:24.152 --> 00:07:26.863
And even if Biden maintains 
popular support to send weapons

00:07:26.863 --> 00:07:28.239
he might not have enough.

00:07:28.239 --> 00:07:30.450
A recent analysis found that certain supplies

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.034
may be approaching the minimal level 

00:07:32.034 --> 00:07:34.245
that the US requires for 
its own war planning.

00:07:35.204 --> 00:07:37.081
It's why the US is now focusing on ways 

00:07:37.081 --> 00:07:39.125
to increase production of weapons
at home

00:07:39.125 --> 00:07:40.751
in allied countries
and in Ukraine.

00:07:41.002 --> 00:07:43.212
But just building those plants 
could take years.

00:07:43.963 --> 00:07:45.631
One year of US and allied support

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:47.300
has helped transform 
the Ukrainian army

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:48.759
into a formidable force

00:07:48.759 --> 00:07:50.344
whose soldiers have repeatedly held

00:07:50.344 --> 00:07:52.346
its ground against a much larger 
Russian army.

00:07:53.681 --> 00:07:56.309
But with both the battlefield and 
US politics changing

00:07:56.309 --> 00:07:58.728
the questions now are how well 
is Ukraine going to fight

00:07:58.728 --> 00:07:59.770
with what they have

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:02.690
and how much more US help can they count on?

00:08:08.488 --> 00:08:10.698
Thanks for watching this episode
of Vox Atlas.

00:08:10.698 --> 00:08:12.783
Vox has been covering the war 
in Ukraine since it began

00:08:12.783 --> 00:08:14.952
with in-depth explainers and analysis.

00:08:15.244 --> 00:08:19.040
You can find all our coverage 
of the war at Vox.com/Ukraine.

